## Certification requirements

Under **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.1 — "Certification Requirements"** ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), effective Jan. 15, 2020), the provider must:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV Case Management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the standards in Sections **6.1.1, 6.1.2, and 6.1.3**;
- ensure supervision by **qualified supervisors** meeting Sections **6.1.4 and 6.1.5**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records and inspect agency and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of proposed changes such as **ownership, name, address, telephone number, or dissolution** within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**; and
- **within one year of Medicaid enrollment**, achieve **national accreditation** from one of the listed accrediting bodies: **ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC**.

### What counts as a “qualified” case manager or supervisor?

The policy cross-references provider qualification sections, so those are part of the certification picture:

- **Case managers** must meet one of the education/experience pathways in **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.1 — "HIV Case Manager"** ([same link](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)). These include, for example:
  - a master’s degree in a human services field;
  - a bachelor’s in social work;
  - a bachelor’s in a human services field with specified coursework plus experience;
  - a bachelor’s degree plus relevant counseling/human services experience; or
  - applicable NC licensure (for certain professionals) plus **two years** of human services experience.

- Case managers must also be able to perform the functions listed in **Section 6.1.2 — "Case Management Experience"**, including assessment, care planning, referral/linkage, monitoring, and documentation with signed attestation.

- They must have the knowledge and abilities listed in **Section 6.1.3 — "Knowledge, Skills and Abilities"**, including documented HIV knowledge **within one year of hire**.

- **Supervisors** must meet one of the qualification pathways in **Section 6.1.4 — "HIV Case Management Supervisor"**, such as certain master’s or bachelor’s degrees with specified human services experience, or applicable licensure plus experience.

- Training requirements appear in **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.1.7 — "Training Requirements"** ([same link](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)):
  - basic training for case managers and supervisors covers HIV information, prevention, scope of work, core components, ethics, system responsibilities, and documentation requirements; and
  - annual training must include confidentiality, cultural competency, current HIV disease management trends, ethics, refresher case management core components, and treatment of people living with HIV. The policy states that **6 of the 12 annual hours** must be **clinically oriented training**.

### Recertification

For continued certification, **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.4 — "Recertification"** ([same link](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)) requires the provider to:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of items from the initial certification materials that have changed;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual summaries of quality improvement activities including **outcome metrics**; and
- submit to a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of records and other documentation as needed.

## Grounds for decertification

Under **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, Section 6.2.5 — "Decertification"** ([Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), effective Jan. 15, 2020), a provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- **failure to provide core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to **develop, submit, and implement a written plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do **not meet Section 6.1 criteria**;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing educational units** under Section 6.1;
- failure to provide case management staff with **supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs**;
- failure to submit required documentation within the time frame required by the policy, NC Medicaid, or the designated contractor;
- failure to maintain documentation under **Section 7.5** sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of agency changes (name, director, ownership, address, phone) so that the agency becomes unreachable;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in **Section 6.2**.

## Bottom line

In plain terms, an HIV case management provider must show:

- **organizational experience**,
- **qualified and trained staff**,
- **qualified supervision**,
- **site enrollment**,
- **accreditation within one year**,
- **quality improvement and record review compliance**, and
- ongoing **reporting and recertification compliance**.

A provider can be decertified for problems in any of those areas, especially **fraud, records issues, confidentiality breaches, unqualified staff, missed training, lack of supervision, inadequate documentation, failure to correct deficiencies, or failure to maintain certification standards**.

